<figure class="durability-state">
 <p class="figure-title">Crash after a durable acknowledgement, before this heap page is written</p>
 <div class="states">
  <section><p class="panel-title">Memory · lost in the crash</p><div class="page"><b>Buffered heap page</b><span>Version with stock 1</span></div><p>Separate transaction state: W committed.</p></section>
  <section><p class="panel-title">Surviving storage</p><div class="page"><b>Earlier heap page</b><span>Still contains stock 3</span></div><ol><li>WAL for the row change</li><li>WAL commit record for W</li></ol><p>Required WAL was flushed before success.</p></section>
  <section><p class="panel-title">Restart</p><p>Replay needed page changes from WAL.</p><div class="page rebuilt"><b>Recovered heap page</b><span>Replacement with stock 1</span></div><p>W’s committed status makes the replacement visible to a new read.</p></section>
 </div>
 <figcaption>This is one possible ordering with ordinary logged tables and reliable local durability settings. A data page may instead be written earlier, provided its required WAL is already durable. The acknowledgement does not require every modified data page to have been written.</figcaption>
</figure>
